Today, our nation stands at the ballot box in the 2026 General Election after a campaign period that has been notably peaceful by historical standards, yet marred by the familiar patterns of unverified accusations, political grandstanding, and officials speaking with forked tongues about the same institutions. As Zambians exercise their democratic right, the claims and counterclaims from political players deserve our careful scrutiny rather than passive consumption.

## Main Stories

1. **Zambia Votes in 2026 General Election**
   President Hakainde Hichilema cast his ballot at Kabulonga Boys Secondary School in Lusaka on Thursday, urging all registered voters to turn out before polls close at 6 p.m. and to bring their green national registration card and voter's card. His emphasis that "the decision for the next five years rests where it should—with the people of Zambia" stands in notable contrast to Vice-President Mutale Nalumango's remarks the previous day, when she warned the Tonse Alliance they would "cry" and should "go to your homes and cry" after losing—a rhetorical flourish that sits awkwardly alongside the President's call for peaceful, inclusive democracy.

2. **Electoral Commission Promises Results Within 48–72 Hours**
   The Electoral Commission of Zambia expects to receive all constituency results at the totalling centre within 48 hours after the last ballot is cast, with Chairperson Mwangala Zaloumis calling for patience and accuracy during announcement. ECZ Director of ICT Dr Collins Kachaka indicated a 72-hour window for final declaration, which raises the question of whether the 48-hour or 72-hour timeframe applies—both were mentioned in the same presentation, and clarity matters when a nation is waiting. The ECZ's capacity to deliver timely, transparent results will be tested